        {
            "id": "irrational-numbers",
            "title": "Irrational Numbers & Proofs",
            "icon": "🔍",
            "precheck": {
                "question": "Which of these cannot be written as $p/q$?",
                "options": [
                    "0.333...",
                    "$\\sqrt{2}$",
                    "5/7"
                ],
                "correctIndex": 1,
                "passMessage": "Correct! $\\sqrt{2}$ has a non-terminating, non-repeating expansion.",
                "failMessage": "0.333... is 1/3 (rational). $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational."
            },
            "learn": {
                "paragraphs": [
                    "**1. Irrational Numbers:** A number that cannot be expressed as $p/q$. Examples: $\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{3}, \\pi$, and $0.1010010001...$",
                    "**2. Proof by Contradiction:** This is a powerful logical procedure (Algorithmic Thinking) where we assume the opposite of what we want to prove and find a 'clash' or contradiction."
                ],
                "decomposition": [
                    "To prove $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational, we break the logic into: 1. Assume it IS rational. 2. Show this implies $p$ and $q$ are both even. 3. Contradict the 'simplest form' rule."
                ],
                "algorithms": [
                    {
                        "steps": [
                            "**Proof of Irrationality of $\\sqrt{2}$:**",
                            "Assume $\\sqrt{2} = p/q$ in simplest form.",
                            "Square both sides: $2 = p^2/q^2 \\Rightarrow p^2 = 2q^2$.",
                            "This means $p^2$ is even, so $p$ must be even ($p=2k$).",
                            "Substitute: $(2k)^2 = 2q^2 \\Rightarrow 4k^2 = 2q^2 \\Rightarrow q^2 = 2k^2$.",
                            "This means $q^2$ is even, so $q$ must be even.",
                            "If both $p, q$ are even, $p/q$ wasn't in simplest form! **Contradiction!**"
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "boxes": [
                    {
                        "type": "warning",
                        "html": "The sum or product of a rational and an irrational is always irrational."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "practice": [
                {
                    "question": "Is $\\sqrt{9}$ irrational?",
                    "options": [
                        "Yes",
                        "No (It's 3)"
                    ],
                    "correctIndex": 1,
                    "solution": "Root of a perfect square is rational."
                }
            ],
            "test": [
